scope: |
  Owner's decision, and a pragmatic one: `println(s.charAt(0))` must print the CHARACTER on every
  backend, while code that treats the result as a number keeps working -- coerce where needed
  rather than convert the world.
  The consensus already exists in the v2 kernel: `CharV extends IntV`, so a Char IS its code point
  for every numeric site and carries the character for the text sites (`v2-char-is-an-int` solved
  this class for literals). `charAt` simply still returns a bare IntV. That is the minimal change.
  v1 is already right. Rust is the hard lane: its emitted code is typed i64, so a Char there needs a
  newtype that Displays as the character and compares/coerces as a number -- the same total-coercion
  approach as the Any boundary, not a rewrite.
next: v2 charAt -> CharV, measure all lanes, then rust
